  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Evaluating the Dashboard**\n\nI\'m incredibly impressed! The EditorView is truly exceptional, flawlessly integrating with the API options. Now, I\'m shifting focus to the user\'s first image: the **\355\217\211\352\260\200 \353\214\200\354\213\234\353\263\264\353\223\234** (Evaluation Dashboard).\n\n\n**Constructing the Comparison**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in developing the **ComparisonView.tsx** page. It\'s envisioned as a compelling dashboard view that juxtaposes creative outputs from two distinct author personas (e.g., a movie script model versus a novelist model) in a 2-column grid. I\'m focusing on a dark UI theme for visual contrast, as per design guidelines. The title will be \"\353\271\204\352\265\220 \353\266\204\354\204\235\" (Comparison Analysis) with a customizable subtitle linked to the prompt. A \"Export Data\" button will also be provided.\n\n\n**Designing the UI Elements**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the UI elements for the ComparisonView. I will be incorporating distinct card elements for each model\'s output, with headers and bodies containing text from each model. Column 2, will use \"LITERARY_DARK\" as the LORA header and a corresponding text. On the right, an evaluation panel will contain metrics on style consistency, genre fit, and coherence, along with risk indicators for persona contamination and cliche density, and a diagnostic log section with simulated console output. Finally, a prompt exchange button will be available to allow real-time model comparison.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 19
      nanos: 381094817
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
